WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.300
Imagine an inspection of a 300-foot communications tower beside a road.

2
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:10.280
The aircraft powers on, the controller shows satellites, and the camera feed looks sharp.

3
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:18.620
A remote pilot still has to ask whether the small UAS is in a condition for safe operation and whether the planned flight remains safe as conditions change.

4
00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.880
A technically functioning drone is not automatically suitable for the mission.

5
00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:35.700
Loose propellers, a damaged battery, a weak control link, a new payload, rising wind, or moving traffic nearby can turn a routine job into a hazardous operation.

6
00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:45.820
Part 107 requires the remote pilot to check the aircraft before flight and to stop continuing a flight when the aircraft is no longer in a safe condition.

7
00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:52.160
It also prohibits careless or reckless operation and prohibits dropping an object in a way that creates an undue hazard.

8
00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:55.220
The practical meaning is broader than a checklist item.

9
00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:01.480
The remote pilot is managing the energy, position, and failure path of an aircraft.

10
00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:08.060
If a clamp, filter, or payload could detach over people or vehicles, the launch decision changes.

11
00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.820
Visual line of sight is central.

12
00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:31.320
With unaided vision other than corrective lenses, the remote pilot, the person manipulating the controls, or a visual observer must be able to see the unmanned aircraft throughout the flight well enough to know its location, attitude, altitude, direction of flight, surrounding air traffic, nearby hazards, and whether it endangers life or property.

13
00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:42.860
A controller screen, map display, telemetry readout, first-person-view goggles, or camera feed can support awareness, but none independently replaces the required visual capability.

14
00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:53.040
A distant tower bolt visible in the camera may be beyond the pilot's ability to keep the aircraft visually oriented, especially against trees, wires, or glare.

15
00:01:53.320 --> 00:02:00.120
The aircraft must yield the right of way to crewed aircraft and may not pass over, under, or ahead unless well clear.

16
00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:04.060
That rule controls collision risk, not etiquette.

17
00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:13.200
If a helicopter enters the area, the remote pilot should descend, land, hold clear, or otherwise maneuver early enough to avoid creating a conflict.

18
00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:20.100
A visual observer can help, but the crew must communicate effectively and the remote pilot in command remains accountable.

19
00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:23.980
The numeric operating limits also have operational purpose.

20
00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:28.640
Groundspeed may not exceed 87 knots, or 100 miles per hour.

21
00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:39.940
The normal altitude limit is 400 feet above ground level, with a structure exception that allows flight within a 400-foot radius of a structure and no higher than 400 feet above the structure's immediate uppermost limit.

22
00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:44.760
Minimum flight visibility from the control station is 3 statute miles.

23
00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:52.060
The aircraft must remain at least 500 feet below clouds and 2,000 feet horizontally from clouds.

24
00:02:52.160 --> 00:03:00.240
These limits protect see-and-avoid capability, collision avoidance, recovery margin, and predictability for other airspace users.

25
00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:03.320
Other limits affect launch design.

26
00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:07.020
Part 107 bars operation from a moving aircraft.

27
00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:20.080
Operation from a moving land or water vehicle is allowed only in the rule's limited conditions, including sparsely populated areas and no carriage of another person's property for compensation or hire.

28
00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:23.380
The aircraft may not carry hazardous material.

29
00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:34.680
Crew members must comply with alcohol and drug rules, and no one should serve while illness, fatigue, medication, stress, or another condition interferes with safe operation.

30
00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:43.740
For the tower beside the road, the safe answer may be a shorter orbit, a lower altitude, a closed takeoff area, a delayed launch, or a refusal.

31
00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:51.520
The pilot's job is to keep the aircraft inside a safe operating envelope, not to use every inch of the legal envelope.
